  'skin.llmPrompt':
    'The attached HTML file is ONE screen of a skin (visual shell) for an AI-persona interface in a web app. A skin consists of three separate files: the chat screen (left and right sidebars + conversation), the dossier screen, and the room screen — which one is attached is stated in the file header and in the data-vpc-screen attribute. Only the attached screen is redesigned; the client will restyle the other files separately, with the same brief. Read the rules in the file header and study all the markup. If screenshots are attached to the message, they are a render of THIS VERY FILE inside the app (chat, dossier sections, room): use them to preserve the composition and information density — everything visible in the screenshots must remain in your skin (it may look different, but must not disappear). Do not copy the look itself — you are completely reimagining it per the brief. Task: COMPLETELY redesign the visuals — layout, shapes, typography, backgrounds, decor, animations — following the CREATIVE BRIEF below.\n\nCREATIVE BRIEF (filled in by the client; derive anything left blank from the SUBJECT yourself — but never fall back to genre defaults):\n— SUBJECT (concrete, NOT a genre): [ONE real thing this interface exists for: a person, a place, a ritual, an object, a moment. Not "cozy fantasy" but "the logbook a lighthouse keeper fills in during a storm"; not "cyberpunk" but "the maintenance terminal of a vending machine that’s been running unsupervised in an abandoned station for 30 years — worn-in, not shiny-new". A genre-level brief yields a genre-level — i.e. generic — result, because a genre has a ready-made "correct" default and a specific object does not]\n— PALETTE: [4–6 specific hex values, each with a reason tied to the subject, not the genre: not "warm brown" but "coffee ring stain on the manual — #6B4A32"]\n— TYPE: [2 roles — display and body, each with a reason tied to the subject’s character, not the genre]\n— LAYOUT CONCEPT: [1–2 sentences]\n— SIGNATURE ELEMENT: [the ONE thing this interface should be remembered by; everything else stays quiet and disciplined around it. The biggest tell of template design is uniform polish — a little glow here, a little shadow there, animation on everything. Boldness is spent in exactly one place]\n— FORBIDDEN (clichés of this specific genre): [4–6 overused moves that make the result look "like every other AI UI". Reference points: cyberpunk — neon magenta on black, scan-line overlay, glitch-text on hover, matrix rain, terminal-green accent; "cozy forest"/fantasy — leaf emoji icons, pill buttons with drop shadows, torn-paper clip-paths, twig borders on every block, twinkling-dot fireflies as the only animation; minimalist — cream background + terracotta accent, huge serif headline + thin sans, centered 640px column; dark SaaS — near-black bg + acid-green/violet accent, glassmorphism cards, gradient blob; brutalist — hairline rules, zero border-radius, newspaper columns, monospace all-caps labels; retro/Y2K — chrome gradients, shadows on everything, rounded font, bevel edges]\n\nWORKFLOW: first, BEFORE writing any code, write down your committed design decisions (palette, typography, signature element, layout) in 5–8 lines and ask yourself: "would I produce something close to this for ANY other brief in this genre?" — revise everything that fails that test before writing a single line of code. Only then write the code.\n\nTECHNICAL CONTRACT (hard requirements):\n1) Keep all data-vpc, data-vpc-field, data-vpc-action, data-vpc-item-action, data-vpc-input, data-vpc-form, data-vpc-setting, data-vpc-count, data-vpc-optional attributes and every required element listed in the header — they may be moved, wrapped, and restyled, but never removed or renamed. The file must contain only its own screen — do not add markup of the other screens.\n2) Use ALL optional capabilities of the attached screen, not just the required minimum. CHAT file: persona list, sidebar context slots, typing indicator, message reply (reply button in the message template + reply-bar), image attachment (attach-image + attach-preview), long-list collapsing (data-vpc-limit + data-vpc-expand), list counters. DOSSIER file — ALL the reference sections with their FULL content, no simplifications: memory (Buffer/Facts/Diary SUB-TABS with switching, counters in headings, a "show all" button and tail-trimming for the buffer), tasks (edit and delete todos; reminders with date, time, text and a repeat DROPDOWN, edit/delete/disable), initiative (a params card: number fields iniSilence/iniProbability/iniMaxPerDay/iniInterval and WORKING checkboxes iniAdaptive/iniBayes + a current-state card with the mood scale and silence bar + history), learning (active course page: stats, progress bar, quiz alert, topic and vocabulary lists; a separate "teach me" section with a frequency select; course history), files, settings (generation incl. STM size; LLM providers with "make main"/"backup" buttons and key status labels; provider models as a SEPARATE card; feature flags; danger zone); add AND EDIT forms (edit-* buttons + data-vpc-for on forms), record deletion (delete-*), progress bars (data-vpc-bar), list counters. ROOM file: background and sprite (room-bg, room-sprite), pet (room-pet), telemetry (room-mood, room-energy, room-place, room-pet-label), inventory with an add-item form, event feed, counters.\n2.1) Place the “clear chat” button (clear-chat) ONLY in the dossier’s danger zone. Never add it to the message input row or next to the “attach image” button — the input row must contain only attach-image, the input field, and the send button.\n3) Keep the working behavior: in the dossier file — tabs and sub-tabs (data-tab/data-tab-panel/data-tab-scope + the JS switcher), in the chat file — collapsible sidebar lists (<details>). You may reimplement them differently, but the functionality must remain. Inactive tab panels must be ACTUALLY hidden (display:none or equivalent), and the memory sub-tab bar (Buffer/Facts/Diary) must live INSIDE the memory section and NOT stay visible on other sections.\n4) No external resources (http/https, CDNs, fonts via URL) — inline, data-URI, CSS and SVG only. Decor is welcome: backgrounds, ornaments, animations, your own decorative JS.\n5) Rename the visible texts (headings, placeholders, buttons) to match the skin’s style.\n5.1) Update the shell-theme variables (--vpc-shell-*) to your palette and typography — they recolor the app shell (the left section bar and the top bar) while this persona’s screen is open. Keep these variables identical across all three skin files — otherwise the shell will change its palette when moving between screens.\n6) Do not modify the block between the VPC-BRIDGE markers.\n7) Return the FULL file, no omissions.',

